.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-GET-USER 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-get-user \- show one user account
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam get-user
.BR \-\-user\-id | \-u
.I ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows one user as JSON: the account and region they belong to, whether
they may log in, their access keys and when they were created.
.PP
The same description
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-users (1)
gives of each of its own, for one user \(em so what a listing shows and
what this shows cannot drift apart.
.PP
By user id, which is what everything else names a user with: a grant's
principal, an application's technical identity, the
.I userId
the audit trail records. An application's principal is its
.I applicationId
prefixed with
.IR app\- ,
so
.B "eam get-user \-u app\-parser"
is how to look up what the
.I parser
application runs as.
.PP
Requires administrator privileges. A user's own record is not an
exception: this is the administrative view of somebody else, and a
principal that could read it for itself could enumerate the
installation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user\-id " " \fIID\fR
Id of the user to show. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The user was found and printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no user with that id, the caller is not an
administrator, or the server could not be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eam get\-user \-\-user\-id jvo

# what an application runs as
euclid\-cli eam get\-user \-\-user\-id app\-parser
.fi
.RE
.PP
The grants that go with it are a separate question, because this shows
the user and not what it may do:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eam list\-grants \e
    \-\-principal ern:eam:eu\-central\-1:000000000000:user:app\-parser
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-users (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-user (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-user-group (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1)
